   "id": 503164,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "<img src=\"exercises/media/ec06c9953d6d727d.jpg\" alt=\"\">",
   "stem_html": "This image shows the chemical structure of the R and S forms of the drug thalidomide. The dark triangle represents that bond coming toward you out of the page, and the dashed triangle represents that bond going away from you into the page. The drug can spontaneously switch between these two forms. How do you think that affects the drug's function?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 988787,
     "content_html": "It does not affect the drug's function. These are enantiomers (mirror images) and therefore react the same."
    },
    {
     "id": 988788,
     "content_html": "The R form is trans and the S form is cis. The R form will have a higher melting point and therefore not be absorbed as efficiently as the S form."
    },
    {
     "id": 988789,
     "content_html": "The  R form is trans and the S form is cis. The S form will compete with cholesterol and disrupt cellular membrane structure, causing adverse side effects."
    },
    {
     "id": 988790,
     "content_html": "These are enantiomers (mirror images). The mixture of both enantiomers could be less effective  than the pure form of one enantiomer, or even harmful, because the two forms can react differently."
    }
   ],
